CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ two steps: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ one step: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [temp.spec.partial.match]/1, [temp.constr.decl]/1: impl<int, 2>
derives from impl<int, 3>, which derives from impl<int, 4>, an instance of
the constrained partial specialization.  The front end failed silently
("could not fully type-check 'main'", bin53): elaborate_class_template cut
off recursion of a template after two enclosing instances unless the
arguments converge, and for a constrained partial specialization (re-matched
from the primary) it counted the primary's instances, so impl<int, 4> was
never instantiated and impl<int, 3> lost its base.  Each partial
specialization is a template of its own ([temp.spec.partial.general]/1);
the bound now counts the specialization's instances.  test_unsatisfied.desc:
unsatisfied constrained specializations for B = 1 and 2; when none matched,
the primary was instantiated with the specialization's arguments, B unbound,
and impl<int, B + 1> named impl<int, 2> itself (crash on bin53, "a class
cannot be derived from itself" since round 163t).  This was the root cause
of cpp20_common_reference_int_lvalue_rvalue failing with the libstdc++ 16
headers.  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept both programs, and they fail only
their WRONG assertion.
